# Production environment file. Copy to .env.prod on the server and fill in real values.
# NEVER commit .env.prod — it holds secrets. (.env* is already gitignored.)
#
#   cp .env.prod.example .env.prod
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod pull
#   docker compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml --env-file .env.prod up -d
#
# EVERY EMPTY VALUE BELOW NEEDS ONE. They ship empty rather than filled with an example because a
# plausible-looking value is a valid one: it deploys, and then fails somewhere far from here — a
# certificate that never issues, a password published in this repository, password-reset links
# pointing at a domain you do not own.
#
# They do not all fail in the same place, which is worth knowing before your first deploy:
#
#   Refused by Compose, before an image is pulled — so `docker compose config` checks your file
#   without touching the server:  GHCR_OWNER, IMAGE_TAG, ODYSSEY_DOMAIN, MARIADB_ROOT_PASSWORD,
#   MARIADB_PASSWORD.
#
#   Refused by the migrations job, on a fresh database, with the API held down behind it:
#   BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL and BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD.
#
#   Nothing about mail is refused anywhere any more (issue #8), and that is a real change in
#   posture, not a tidy-up. Mail is configured at /settings after the first sign-in, so a
#   deployment now STARTS with mail switched off and sends nothing until you configure it. Read
#   "Mail transport" below before your first deploy — the window has one consequence you cannot
#   recover from by yourself.
#
# Most of Odyssey's configuration is NOT in this file. Anything an administrator can reasonably
# change without a redeploy lives in the database and is edited at /settings once you are signed in;
# see the last section. What remains here is what must be true before the app can start.
#
# Read docs/deployment.md alongside this file, especially "Backups": the dataprotection_keys volume
# is secret-bearing, must be backed up SEPARATELY from the database dump, and is destroyed by
# `docker compose down -v` — permanently, for anything derived from it.

# ── Release selection ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The GitHub user or org that owns the published images (the <owner> in ghcr.io/<owner>/odyssey-api).
GHCR_OWNER=
# The release to deploy, e.g. 1.4.0 — see the repository's Releases page. Bump it and re-run pull+up
# to upgrade. Pin an exact version rather than "latest" so you decide when schema migrations run.
IMAGE_TAG=

# ── Public URL / TLS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The domain Caddy serves and requests a Let's Encrypt certificate for. It must already resolve
# (A/AAAA) to this server with ports 80 and 443 reachable, or issuance fails and repeated attempts
# count against Let's Encrypt's rate limits.
ODYSSEY_DOMAIN=

# ── Database ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Generate each password separately — do not reuse one for the other:
#   openssl rand -base64 36
# Nothing reads MARIADB_ROOT_PASSWORD except MariaDB itself, so no application-side check can catch
# a weak one.
MARIADB_ROOT_PASSWORD=
MARIADB_USER=odyssey
MARIADB_PASSWORD=
# One EF context owns the whole schema — identity, finance and journal are a single model with real
# foreign keys between them — so there is one database and it cannot be split.
#
# KEEP THE TWO NAMES IDENTICAL. They are different contracts that happen to name the same database:
# MARIADB_DATABASE is read by the MariaDB image's entrypoint, which creates that database on first
# start, and ODYSSEY_DATABASE goes into the connection string both .NET hosts use. Change one only
# and the migrations job connects to a database that was never created.
MARIADB_DATABASE=odyssey
ODYSSEY_DATABASE=odyssey

# ── The first administrator ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required. Registration order grants nobody any privilege, so without these there is no way in:
# the migrations job creates this account, and refuses to finish if it cannot, which holds the API
# down behind it.
#
# The password must satisfy the app's policy (16+ characters, upper + lower + digit + symbol) and is
# a ONE-TIME secret — the account can do nothing until it is changed at first sign-in. Blank the
# line again afterwards; it is read only while the user table is empty, so keeping it here just
# leaves a real credential on disk and in `docker inspect`. For the same reason it is not a password
# reset: a forgotten password goes through the forgot-password flow, which needs working mail.
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_EMAIL=
BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ── Mail transport ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOTHING. There is no mail configuration in this file (issue #8). The relay host, port and STARTTLS
# mode, the public origin every link points at, the sender identity, the send throttle and the relay
# username and password are all configured at /settings → Email once you are signed in, and each
# takes effect on the next send with no redeploy.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE YOUR FIRST DEPLOY. The API no longer refuses to start without a relay, because a
# value entered through the settings UI cannot be a precondition for that UI coming up. So a fresh
# deployment starts with MAIL OFF: every confirmation and password-reset message is logged and
# skipped until you configure SMTP.
#
# The consequence, which is the part with no way back:
#
#   You sign in with BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD above and then configure SMTP. If that one-time
#   password is lost during that window there is NO SELF-SERVICE RECOVERY — the forgot-password
#   flow is exactly the thing that needs working mail — and the account has to be repaired
#   directly in the database.
#
# So: configure mail immediately after your first sign-in, before you blank BOOTSTRAP_ADMIN_PASSWORD,
# and send yourself a password reset to prove the whole path works. Nothing short of a real send
# checks the relay credential, and nothing checks it at startup any more.
#
# Why the transport moved when issue #421 had deliberately kept it here: the sender connects and THEN
# authenticates, so an administrator who could edit the host could point it at a server of their own
# and receive the stored relay credential. That is now closed structurally rather than by keeping the
# value out of reach — changing the host, or turning STARTTLS off, CLEARS the stored SMTP username
# and password in the same transaction, so there is nothing left to present to the new host. You will
# have to re-enter the credential after either change; that is the control working, not a fault.

# ── Upload size ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The TRANSPORT ceiling only — the Kestrel request-body limit and the multipart reader, both fixed
# at startup. The cap users are validated against is set at /settings and cannot exceed this number.
#
# Lowering what users can upload needs no change here; do it at /settings. RAISING it means raising
# three hops, OUTERMOST FIRST: `request_body max_size` in ./Caddyfile, then `client_max_body_size`
# in Odyssey.Client/nginx.conf, then this. See docs/deployment.md, "Upload size: three ceilings, in
# order". The default is 64 MiB, with the two proxy hops at 65 MB for multipart envelope headroom.
FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=67108864

# ── Fixed by the production overlay ──────────────────────────────────────────
# docker-compose.prod.yml pins all three in the container environment, so editing them here does
# nothing. They are listed because what they guarantee is worth knowing: no Development environment
# and no published Swagger UI, and — the dangerous one — no in-memory database, which nothing in the
# app refuses in Production and which would silently discard every write on container restart.
ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production
SWAGGER_ENABLED=false
USE_IN_MEMORY_DATABASE=false
# Demo data is never seeded outside Development/Testing, and the overlay pins this off as well. It
# would create four accounts — one of them an administrator — sharing a password published in this
# repository's README.
SEED_DEMO_DATA=false

# ── Not in this file: everything you set at /settings ────────────────────────
# Sign in as the bootstrap administrator, then:
#
#   Settings -> Credentials    The five secrets, encrypted at rest with the Data Protection keys.
#                              The SMTP username and password (until both are set, no mail is sent
#                              at all); the Anthropic API key for file analysis; the email recipient
#                              hash key; and the legal pseudonymisation salt. The last two are
#                              DERIVATION keys — generate with `openssl rand -base64 48`, and note
#                              that losing one, or losing the dataprotection_keys volume that
#                              decrypts it, cannot be recovered by re-entering a new value. The
#                              pseudonymisation salt is not needed until the first account deletion,
#                              which is where its absence is reported.
#
#   Settings -> Email          The "from" address and display name. Until set, mail goes out as
#                              no-reply@odyssey.local, which no relay will accept — set it before
#                              inviting anyone.
#
#   Settings -> Registration   Whether new accounts need administrator approval and email
#                              confirmation.
#
#   Settings -> File analysis  Statement and receipt extraction, which sends uploaded documents to
#                              Anthropic. It ships OFF and there is no variable here to turn it on:
#                              enabling third-party processing of your users' financial documents is
#                              a decision an operator should make deliberately, with the API key in
#                              place, and be able to reverse without a redeploy.
#
# Plus upload caps, mail throttling and the rest — the page is the inventory. Two file-analysis
# knobs stay out of both places: the prompt template is a deployed file (a version string that can
# drift from the file it names is worse than one that cannot) and the request timeout is consumed
# once at startup by the resilience handler, where a runtime value could never reach a live client.
